description:
CLAIM, made in three places, witnessed behaviourally by nothing: codegen.Finalise is fail-closed — a format.Source rejection returns (nil, err) wrapped in ErrFormatFailure and no raw bytes reach the caller. The claim is load-bearing prose in internal/codegen/emit.go's doc comment, in docs/specs/codegen-sentinel-taxonomy.md §4, and in merged PR #2589's whole justification for deleting listEncoder's indent argument ("there is no path that writes these bytes unformatted").

FALSIFIER, run on sweep gqlc-46s2b at base 6974045b in a scratch clone:

Pass 1 — naive fail-open rewrite (`return nil, ...` → `formatted = f.Contents`): KILLED, but by TestSentinelTaxonomy/TestExcludedBranchesAreUnreached, a census that fails because "no branch in package codegen returns it". That is a source-shape kill: the census asks whether a branch RETURNING the sentinel exists and goes unexecuted; it cannot distinguish fail-closed from fail-open.

Pass 2 — the same fail-open rewrite with a dead branch kept to satisfy the census:

    if err != nil {
        if len(files) < 0 {
            return nil, fmt.Errorf("%w: %s: %w", ErrFormatFailure, f.Path, err)
        }
        formatted = f.Contents
    }

Screen: `go test -c -o /dev/null ./internal/codegen/` exit 0. Verdict: SURVIVED over the FULL module — ./internal/codegen/... (6 packages incl. the conformance corpus) plus the remaining 23 packages, `go test -count=1`, exit 0, zero failures. Sandbox limit: the nested test/data/codegen module was not run; it lints emitted goldens and holds no Finalise caller.

WHY IT MATTERS: unformattable emission is always unparseable Go, so fail-open means a template bug writes a broken file into the user's tree instead of refusing with a named path. TestUpdateCannotPreserveGoldensOnFailure covers the -update wipe ordering but drives a GENERATION failure (unaliased expression), never the format arm. Taxonomy §4 deliberately declined a corpus FIXTURE for ErrFormatFailure ("a fixture for it would buy a test seam rather than coverage") — that reasoning is sound and this bead does not dispute it, but it covers fixtures only.

REMEDY, already shaped: a direct unit test of the exported Finalise in internal/codegen — no fixture, no seam. Feed []File{{Path: "a.go", Contents: []byte("package \x01")}, {Path: "b.go", Contents: valid}} and assert (nil, error), errors.Is ErrFormatFailure, error text names a.go, and the returned slice is nil rather than partial. That test kills the pass-2 mutant by construction; re-run the rows above to confirm.

No from-pr label: the arm predates any single PR I can pin, and decision 0004 §3 says omit rather than guess. PR #2589 did not introduce this — it is the merge that leans on it, which is how sweep found it.

Found by the mutation battery on gqlc-5utdh (the age test-package conversion), as the one row of 33 that survived.

MEASURED, not inferred: with `ElemDecoder = elemDecoder` in export_test.go rewritten to `ElemDecoder = decodeFunc` — the two share the type func(string) string — the whole of `go test ./internal/codegen/age` stays green. 37 top-level tests, all passing. So no test in this package can tell the two functions apart.

WHY THEY ARE USUALLY THE SAME. render_models.go:1272:

    func elemDecoder(elem string) string {
        if agtypeCarrier(elem) == elem {
            return decodeFunc(elem)
        }
        return fmt.Sprintf("func(elem []byte) (%s, error) {...}", elem, decodeFunc(elem), elem)
    }

The first arm IS decodeFunc. The second — the wrapper that converts the carrier into a named element type — is what makes elemDecoder its own function, and it is the arm nothing asserts on directly.

The only direct call site is render_queries_test.go:126, inside 'a slice refuses at its element':

    require.PanicsWithValue(t,
        `age codegen bug: Go type "byte" carries as "byte", which decodeFunc has no arm for`,
        func() { age.ElemDecoder("byte") })

`agtypeCarrier("byte") == "byte"`, so that input takes the first arm and the assertion is satisfied by decodeFunc itself. The subtest's stated claim — that the element's refusal is one level in, at the decoder the wrapper is written with — is true but is not pinned to elemDecoder by this row: it would read identically if elemDecoder had been deleted and its call sites pointed at decodeFunc.

WHAT WOULD CLOSE IT: a row whose element is a named type the table carries as something else, so agtypeCarrier(elem) != elem and the wrapping arm is taken, asserting on the emitted wrapper text. That row would also give the existing subtest a partner that fails when the two functions are confused.

NOT a defect the conversion introduced. Before gqlc-5utdh the same test called `elemDecoder(\"byte\")` unqualified and the same equivalence held; the conversion only made it visible, because a bridged alias is a thing a battery can point somewhere else. Deliberately not fixed on that branch — scope discipline.

`internal/codegen/age/render_models_test.go` declares:

    // propertyWidths is every property type this backend carries, one row
    // per emitted Go type. Taken from the property table rather than written
    // out here, so a width the table gains is a row these sweeps gain.
    var propertyWidths = []graph.PropertyType{
        graph.TypeString, graph.TypeBool, graph.TypeInt32, graph.TypeFloat32,
        graph.TypeAnyPropertyValue, graph.ListOf(graph.TypeInt64, false),
        graph.TypeTimestamp,
    }

The comment says the rows come from the table. They are a hand-written list of seven, and the comment's consequence — 'a width the table gains is a row these sweeps gain' — does not hold: a width added to `typeMap.Property` gains no row here, and the two sweeps built on it (`TestZoneIsMarkedOnlyBesideTheInstant`, `TestEmittedHelpersAreClosedOverWhatTheyCall`) go on covering the same seven.

The same doc-comment sentence is repeated on `TestZoneIsMarkedOnlyBesideTheInstant` ('taken from the property table rather than written out here, so a width the table gains is a row this sweep gains'), so there are two copies of the false claim.

Both sweeps are about emission closure — that a batch carrying one width marks every helper it names. A width the table gains without a row is exactly the case they exist for, and it is the case they cannot see.

## Fix

Either derive the rows from the table, or say plainly that the list is hand-written and what keeps it honest.

There is now a deriving reader to reuse: `typeTableGoTypes` in `render_queries_test.go` (landed on gqlc-u039) reads every Go type text `typeMap` can name out of types.go's AST. These sweeps want `graph.PropertyType` values rather than Go type texts, so the reuse is not direct, but the AST-over-copy posture is.

Found during gqlc-u039's second-copy sweep.
